{"study_accession":"PRJNA1228071","secondary_study_accession":"SRP566244","title":"IFN-γ induction of GBP1 drives aberrant macrophage activation in human skin granulomas","description":"Granuloma annulare (GA) and cutaneous sarcoidosis (cSAR), non-communicable skin conditions, possess an overlapping immunopathology, in which the aberrant activation of macrophages by IFN-γ constitutes a central driver of disease. Nevertheless, our understanding of the molecular events occurring in pathologically activated GA and cSAR macrophages remains limited. Here, we analyzed published single-cell RNA sequencing data of GA and cSAR and performed in-vitro experiments with primary human cells, allowing us to show that oxidative phosphorylation (OXPHOS) is a dominant metabolic pathway in IFN-γ-activated macrophages. Furthermore, we identify an IFN-γ-induced, OXPHOS-sensitive response network in human GA and cSAR macrophages. Within this network, GBP1 plays a central role in controlling IFN-γ-mediated macrophage activation. Meanwhile, inhibition of OXPHOS and GBP1 both promoted resolution of granulomas and prevented granuloma induction in a human in-vitro granuloma model. Taken together, our study suggests that OXPHOS and GBP1 represent therapeutic strategies for treating cutaneous granulomatous diseases.","pubmed":"42213834","geo_series":"","n_samples":47,"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMN47006348","study_accession":"PRJNA1228071","blood_class":"pbmc","class_from":"sample","title":"Glc_LPS_IFNg_6","description":"Glc_LPS_IFNg_6","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM8814871","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2026-04-23","attributes":[["source_name","PBMC"],["cell_type","Monocyte-derived Macrophage"],["treatment","IFNg+LPS"],["donor","6"],["media","RPMI with 10mM Glucose"],["tissue","PBMC"],["geo_loc_name","missing"],["collection_date","missing"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR32484454","experiment_accession":"SRX27798376","library_layout":"PAIRED","library_selection":"cDNA",